 minerals calcium and phosphorus stick them together and what do you get bone of course

2
00:00:19,830 --> 00:00:27,170
 the human skeleton an amazing structure with over 200 separate bones its job is to support your body

3
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:36,560
 and protect its most vulnerable parts the brain the spinal column the lungs the heart

4
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:46,900
 the light framework scarcely 20 of the body's mass if it were made of metal it would weigh four or

5
00:00:46,900 --> 00:01:01,900
 five times more imagine but we're talking of adult bones what about the young skeleton well it has a

6
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:29,570
 lot of gristle or cartilage these are cartilage cells and a control room of one of these cells we

7
00:01:29,570 --> 00:01:52,530
 find the operator hard at work it's only a bad dream the mere thought of an osteoblast really

8
00:01:52,530 --> 00:02:24,300
 gives me the willies and there's a globus and company look at that well dear children it looks

9
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:25,890
 as they were in for some excitement.

10
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:30,000
 Yes, indeed, these cartilage cells are too soft to make a good skeleton.

11
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,840
 Those minerals, phosphates and calcium have come to turn the gristle to bone,

12
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:36,010
 aided and abetted by vitamin D.

13
00:02:36,010 --> 00:02:40,060
 But the cells who go into the front line are the osteoblasts.

14
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:50,560
 When they get here, you'll see what happens.

15
00:02:50,560 --> 00:03:06,320
 No, I just had another dream about those awful osteoblasts.

16
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:12,040
 Not a dream! Red alert! We're under attack!

17
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:18,300
 Listen men, the balloon's gone up! The osteoblasts are on the march!

18
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:21,480
 They are treacherous bone cells detested by loyal cartilage.

19
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:24,780
 But we shall stand fast, they shall not turn us into bone.

20
00:03:25,230 --> 00:03:28,590
 Hard, ugly bone, as hard as stone, is that what you want?

21
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:32,130
 Follow me, my destiny is to lead the cartilage patriots.

22
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:35,750
 We'll fight to the last man, down with a loathsome osteoblast.

23
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:38,550
 So be it death to the osteoblast.

24
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:47,910
 The charge of the osteoblast brigade.

25
00:03:54,380 --> 00:04:02,010
 Vitamin D warriors, aided by phosphorus and calcium, move in to consolidate the territory conquered by the shot tree.

26
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:21,810
 Relentlessly, the attacks are repeated, changing soft cartilage into hard bone.

27
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:25,870
 Irresistible, aren't they?

28
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:29,040
 They've got strength on their side, all right.

29
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:31,310
 Just bone cells?

30
00:04:31,650 --> 00:04:37,890
 It's fortunate that the bone-forming cells always win the battle, because they create a strong, healthy skeleton.

31
00:04:50,840 --> 00:05:00,430
 Completely hardened by now?

32
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:06,190
 The victory of the osteoblast would not be complete for several years, in the course of normal development.

33
00:05:06,190 --> 00:05:13,840
 Bone, like Rome, is not built in a day, and besides, the flexibility of the immature skeleton protects the bones from serious injury.

34
00:05:13,840 --> 00:06:03,040
 My young friend can't live that sort of life with brittle, adult bones, can you?

35
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:14,880
 What you are now observing, children, is a fascinating drama, another battle between osteoblast and osteoclast.

36
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:30,880
 In bone, I don't believe it!

37
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:34,830
 Matching the stuff like salted popcorn!

38
00:06:34,830 --> 00:06:36,540
 Those are the osteoclasts.

39
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:40,240
 An osteoclast breaks down the bone that an osteoblast builds up,

40
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:43,780
 and that's good, because the osteoblasts don't know where to stop.

41
00:06:44,130 --> 00:06:48,390
 The function of the osteoclasts is to act as a counterbalance, you see.

42
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:51,540
 Point them out, thank you.

43
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:55,170
 Those two types of cell actually complement each other.

44
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:57,350
 One builds while the other demolishes.

45
00:06:59,470 --> 00:07:00,710
 Osteoclasts rule, okay?

46
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:05,410
 Without the demolishers, the builders would go on till the whole body was solid bone.

47
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:09,700
 And apart from that, they help us by creating spaces for blood vessels.

48
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:12,190
 Look what they're doing over there.

49
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,300
 The bloodstream can reach the interior of the bone

50
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:19,060
 only through channels dug by those greedy osteoclasts.

51
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:20,560
 Really enormous job, that.

52
00:07:21,430 --> 00:07:22,130
 Vandal!

53
00:07:24,690 --> 00:07:27,720
 You're eating too slowly.

54
00:07:36,110 --> 00:07:38,390
 Again, I haven't even finished it, you swine.

55
00:07:38,730 --> 00:07:40,340
 So sorry about that, you know, mate.

56
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:42,420
 I'm going to finish this job.

57
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:45,810
 You'd better step aside, shorty, my friend.

58
00:07:46,010 --> 00:07:48,030
 Go on, just you try and make me fatso.

59
00:07:48,210 --> 00:07:49,740
 Get him!

60
00:07:52,690 --> 00:08:02,620
 See the paradox? The only way to keep bone healthy is to break it as you build.

61
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:04,510
 Honey!

62
00:08:04,510 --> 00:08:10,370
 So the tiny skeleton grows up.

63
00:08:10,370 --> 00:08:19,930
 As I was saying, boys and girls, today we will study the structure of the human body.

64
00:08:19,930 --> 00:08:24,180
 Let's begin, then, with the bones of the leg.

65
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:27,610
 As soon as Claire's finished daydreaming...

66
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:30,280
 Good, now we can go on.

67
00:08:30,280 --> 00:08:34,800
 Man does not stand on his feet. Instead, he stays upright by means of a balancing act.

68
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:40,800
 Of the 30+ bones in the leg, three of them touch the ground.

69
00:08:41,750 --> 00:08:44,510
 Just standing requires an extremely delicate balance.

70
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,590
 Extremely delicate, that's me.

71
00:08:46,970 --> 00:08:47,370
 Look!

72
00:08:49,230 --> 00:08:52,290
 You sound extremely delicate, Stroppie.

73
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:56,180
 That does it. Get out in the snow, the pair of you.

74
00:08:56,450 --> 00:08:58,140
 That should cool your heels a little, I think.

75
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:08,680
 Listen, that way we can get our own back, Stroppie. What do you think?

76
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:26,300
 Over for today, children.

77
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:28,240
 Now it's time for winter sports.

78
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:41,060
 Keep our delicate balance.

79
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:47,410
 Take your time, I'll keep you company.

80
00:09:52,950 --> 00:09:57,630
 Don't wait for us, you two.

81
00:09:57,810 --> 00:09:58,790
 We'll see you later on.

82
00:09:58,970 --> 00:09:59,230
 Okay.

83
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:05,470
 Your skiing's a lot better, Claire.

84
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:08,710
 Soon you'll be ready for the fast run.

85
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:34,280
 Let's run, weren't we?

86
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:35,620
 Sorry, do you know?

87
00:10:35,740 --> 00:10:37,370
 I always thought it was more to the right.

88
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:54,820
 Before?

89
00:10:56,080 --> 00:11:00,050
 Sir, dear, come back, it's dangerous.

90
00:11:00,390 --> 00:11:01,070
 They can't hear.

91
00:11:01,390 --> 00:11:04,490
 Those two are going to hurt themselves unless I can catch up and warn them in time.

92
00:11:07,740 --> 00:11:17,250
 We're going too fast, it's dangerous, slow down.

93
00:11:59,730 --> 00:12:02,660
 Jumbo, Peter.

94
00:12:03,050 --> 00:12:04,790
 Jumbo, can you go and get us some help?

95
00:12:05,040 --> 00:12:07,230
 Okay, but whatever you do, don't move.

96
00:12:08,200 --> 00:12:09,210
 Bye for now.

97
00:12:09,210 --> 00:12:21,530
 Everybody, and how are we feeling today, children?

98
00:12:21,750 --> 00:12:23,090
 Getting on like a house on fire?

99
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:24,840
 Get your mind at rest, dear lady.

100
00:12:25,160 --> 00:12:27,140
 They'll soon be out of here, believe you me.

101
00:12:27,510 --> 00:12:30,120
 Why, at their age, bones don't take long to knit?

102
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:31,540
 Let's see for yourself.

103
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:34,950
 Simple fracture with no complications.

104
00:12:36,730 --> 00:12:40,130
 Let me assure you, dear lady, that little fracture's nothing to write home about.

105
00:12:40,230 --> 00:12:42,150
 She'll be out of her cast in a matter of weeks.

106
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:44,460
 And my poor son?

107
00:12:45,350 --> 00:12:46,850
 Now, let me see.

108
00:12:47,660 --> 00:12:49,460
 I've got the x-ray with me.

109
00:12:49,560 --> 00:12:50,060
 Here we are.

110
00:12:50,590 --> 00:12:51,840
 But this is serious.

111
00:12:52,370 --> 00:12:54,010
 It's an open compound fracture.

112
00:12:54,330 --> 00:12:55,650
 Now I'm really worried.

113
00:12:56,170 --> 00:12:57,050
 Poor Peter.

114
00:12:57,050 --> 00:12:59,470
 There's absolutely no cause for concern, Nadia.

115
00:12:59,830 --> 00:13:02,580
 The bone has been set and the wound's clean and dressed.

116
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:06,820
 It's an ordinary boot-top fracture, very common on the ski slopes, I'm afraid.

117
00:13:07,290 --> 00:13:08,780
 Why haven't I got a plaster, Doctor?

118
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:12,850
 Because the wound could turn septic under the plaster and we wouldn't know.

119
00:13:13,240 --> 00:13:16,370
 That's why we've got you braced up from the outside.

120
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:17,630
 It's an excellent system.

121
00:13:17,630 --> 00:13:22,530
 Now we can safely leave the process of healing to the best doctor of all, nature.

122
00:13:39,550 --> 00:13:42,810
 Enzymes bring urgently needed calcium to the site of the break.

123
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:47,210
 where the first bridges of soft tissue across the chasm are taking shape.

124
00:13:58,900 --> 00:14:09,770
 Big civil engineering project that we can sabotage over there.

125
00:14:10,030 --> 00:14:54,970
 Follow me when they're needed.

126
00:14:58,870 --> 00:15:00,450
 I'm afraid something's gone wrong.

127
00:15:03,670 --> 00:15:06,440
 Well then, we'll keep our eyes peeled.

128
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:18,100
 He patrolled to all airborne units. Rendezvous at Broken Bone Site.

129
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:20,960
 Philokokas' commando is attacking the bone.

130
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:24,500
 Squadron 012 calling, Lieutenant. We're on our way. Over and out.

131
00:15:27,190 --> 00:15:29,800
 Peter to HQ. We need some armoured units.

132
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:31,970
 Coming, Lieutenant Jumbo.

133
00:15:32,350 --> 00:15:35,110
 Forces, Staphylococci, are attacking the Broken Bone Site.

134
00:15:35,110 --> 00:15:44,850
 Message received, Captain Peter. My men could do with a little bit of active service. Attention! Big double!

135
00:15:51,040 --> 00:16:08,830
 Light fever, I see. Just a few germs trying to make hay in poor old Peter's leg. Don't worry. Sort them out. With an antibiotic. They'll never know what hit them. Come on, young fellow, back up. Relax.

136
00:16:45,240 --> 00:18:40,760
 Maybe vastly outnumbered. Decided to order a strategic withdrawal. Go home.

137
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:45,900
 But begging your pardon, dear friend Stroppy, there must be some place.

138
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:50,520
 A place where we can be safe from all these organisms that keep attacking us everywhere we go.

139
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:51,850
 You idiot.

140
00:18:53,710 --> 00:18:59,270
 Thicker's too short plank of our own because we're parasites.

141
00:18:59,870 --> 00:19:01,990
 Parasites, you bunch of numbskulls, understand?

142
00:19:02,530 --> 00:19:06,900
 We have to borrow a body to live in at first, and then after we've gained control, we're in clover.

143
00:19:07,160 --> 00:19:07,820
 Don't you understand?

144
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:08,760
 We do, we do.

145
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:10,920
 Right moment, numbskulls.

146
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:12,140
 That's all we're waiting for.

147
00:19:12,140 --> 00:19:16,550
 And when the time is ripe, multiply and multiply and become a great nation.

148
00:19:16,670 --> 00:19:17,990
 We'll fulfill our destiny.

149
00:19:18,170 --> 00:19:18,890
 Oh, oh.

150
00:19:25,360 --> 00:19:33,430
 Well, well, what can I say?

151
00:19:33,550 --> 00:19:36,250
 Fancy meeting our old friends here of all places, hmm?

152
00:19:43,420 --> 00:19:55,140
 For the armed forces.

153
00:19:55,700 --> 00:19:56,320
 Yes, indeed.

154
00:19:56,940 --> 00:20:01,070
 At last, we can carry on rebuilding the bone in peace.

155
00:20:02,030 --> 00:20:06,670
 And now I suggest we begin delivering our oxygen to the construction site.

156
00:20:07,030 --> 00:20:08,030
 We're going to need it.

157
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:09,970
 The body repairs itself rapidly.

158
00:20:10,270 --> 00:20:11,070
 Not in my case.

159
00:20:11,350 --> 00:20:12,500
 I'm beyond repair.

160
00:20:14,580 --> 00:20:16,320
 This blood vessel's been cut.

161
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:18,070
 We get lost if we leave it, Professor?

162
00:20:18,370 --> 00:20:19,990
 Never you worry, my child.

163
00:20:20,340 --> 00:20:23,290
 There are alternative routes to get us back into circulation.

164
00:20:24,910 --> 00:20:27,570
 Okay, my girl, you've been up long enough.

165
00:20:28,790 --> 00:20:30,330
 It's easy.

166
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:37,990
 Unlike our children, I've brought you something to nibble.

167
00:20:42,430 --> 00:20:59,940
 We seem to be feeling a great deal better today, don't we?

168
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:03,050
 Yes, Doctor, my son shows signs of improvement.

169
00:21:04,350 --> 00:21:07,850
 Bones seem to be knitting very nicely indeed, young man.

170
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:09,130
 Can I get up now, Doctor?

171
00:21:09,450 --> 00:21:11,110
 Well, we'll have to see about that.

172
00:21:11,150 --> 00:21:12,490
 It's early days yet, you know.

173
00:21:12,870 --> 00:21:16,730
 You'll have to be patient, Peter. After all, that was a compound fracture.

174
00:21:17,440 --> 00:21:21,960
 And if you walk on it again too soon, you might easily do yourself an injury.

175
00:21:22,460 --> 00:21:24,170
 I just hate staying in bed.

176
00:21:24,330 --> 00:21:29,090
 But, Peter, by resting in bed, you're saving the energy your body needs to repair the bone.

177
00:21:29,410 --> 00:21:31,530
 Never put weight on a fracture while it's healing.

178
00:21:32,990 --> 00:21:35,480
 The new bone's still fragile.

179
00:21:35,890 --> 00:21:38,700
 And as for you, young lady, the plaster's coming off.

180
00:21:39,300 --> 00:21:43,200
 Doctor, tell me, does a broken bone ever regain its full strength?

181
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:48,740
 Heavens, yes. In fact, it's a great deal stronger than before at the site of the fracture, my dear lady.

182
00:21:49,300 --> 00:21:54,760
 Strong enough to be the bone in a baby elephant's leg and bear a ton weight, if you can imagine such a thing.

183
00:21:55,220 --> 00:21:56,980
 We can look forward to that.

184
00:21:57,200 --> 00:22:00,000
 Doctor, tell me, what makes a new bone so healthy and strong?

185
00:22:00,700 --> 00:22:02,320
 Oh, it's quite simple, really.

186
00:22:02,740 --> 00:22:08,540
 First of all, the bloodstream delivers all the items needed from outside, like oxygen and minerals.

187
00:22:08,780 --> 00:22:13,560
 And the marrow of the broken bone has an emergency supply of building materials, too.

188
00:22:14,200 --> 00:22:28,450
 And besides, you'd be astonished to learn how much unstable calcium there is just knocking about in the body, as it were.

189
00:22:28,450 --> 00:22:32,450
 There are hormones that steer this calcium to the place where it's needed most.

190
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:15,080
 The healing process continues until one day in spring.

191
00:23:15,080 --> 00:23:21,150
 We seem to be better, don't we?

192
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:24,130
 Children, you're finally going home tomorrow.

193
00:23:24,130 --> 00:23:27,480
 Now remember to take things easy at first, won't you?

194
00:23:27,480 --> 00:23:30,810
 Give me your hand, dear.

195
00:23:30,810 --> 00:23:38,670
 Or near-sure lack of calcium, but you can get it back by eating plenty of cheese and drinking lots of milk.

196
00:23:38,910 --> 00:23:42,510
 I'm quite sure that you'll find this sort of diet most enjoyable.

197
00:23:50,260 --> 00:23:53,780
 And for you, Peter, my boy, I'm going to prescribe lots of sunshine.

198
00:23:54,270 --> 00:24:00,390
 The sunlight will wake up the vitamin D, and the faster the vitamins run with their tiny loads of calcium and phosphorus,

199
00:24:00,390 --> 00:24:03,550
 the faster you'll be on your feet and running, too.

